<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
        Completes the fix colors.xml's comment already describes ("no ... flash on Android
        launch or during the splash → AppShell hand-off") but never finished.

        MainActivity's theme is Maui.SplashTheme (set once, statically, in MainActivity.cs — this
        app never calls SetTheme itself), whose android:windowBackground is the dark branded
        splash drawable. That covers the native splash correctly. But everything the app shows
        AFTER the splash sits on Maui.MainTheme.Base, the SDK-generated base theme this overrides
        — and the generated version never sets android:windowBackground/android:colorBackground,
        so it falls through to its parent, Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight, whose default
        background in light mode is WHITE.

        The gap this produces: once Android considers the splash phase over, the window has
        nothing painted yet except whatever Maui.MainTheme.Base's background resolves to (white),
        until the Shell's first tab — LoanView, a large Syncfusion-heavy page (SfTabView, charts,
        popups) — finishes constructing its native view tree and paints its own themed background
        (an inner Grid's DynamicResource, not the ContentPage itself). Constructing that much
        native content cold is measurably slower on Android than the iOS/Windows equivalent, which
        is why the white screen is Android-only and lasts long enough to notice rather than being
        imperceptible there too.

        Every item below except the two new android:windowBackground/android:colorBackground
        lines is copied VERBATIM from the SDK-generated Maui.MainTheme.Base (as of this build —
        obj/<config>/<tfm>/lp/*/jl/res/values/styles.xml) rather than only setting the two new
        items, because this project style has the SAME name as that generated one: if Android's
        resource merge treats a same-named project style as a full replacement rather than a
        per-item merge, a partial redeclaration here would silently drop everything else that
        theme carries (materialButtonStyle, checkboxStyle, alertDialogTheme, edge-to-edge opt-out,
        ...) — a regression across every native widget in the app that nothing would flag until
        much later. Copying everything makes this safe whichever way the merge actually behaves.

        If a future .NET MAUI SDK upgrade changes what Maui.MainTheme.Base generates, diff this
        against the newly generated styles.xml (same obj/ path) and bring the two new background
        items forward into whatever the new base now contains.
    -->
    <style name="Maui.MainTheme.Base" parent="Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight">
        <item name="maui_edgetoedge_optout">true</item>
        <item name="maui_splash">false</item>
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
        <item name="android:actionMenuTextColor">@color/colorActionMenuTextColor</item>
        <item name="appBarLayoutStyle">@style/MauiAppBarLayout</item>
        <item name="bottomNavigationViewStyle">@style/Widget.Design.BottomNavigationView</item>
        <item name="materialButtonStyle">@style/MauiMaterialButton</item>
        <item name="checkboxStyle">@style/MauiCheckBox</item>
        <item name="android:textAllCaps">false</item>
        <item name="alertDialogTheme">@style/MauiAlertDialogTheme</item>
        <item name="materialAlertDialogTheme">@style/MauiAlertDialogTheme</item>

        <!--  The two lines this override exists for.  -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="android:colorBackground">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
    </style>
</resources>
